                  "app_name": "Slack",
                  "source": "connector",
                  "description": "Slack",
                  "app_key": "zm_slack"
                }
              ],
              "task_prompt": "Fetch the chat messages from the Zoom Chat channel named {{trg1.meeting.meeting_topic}}, keeping only messages sent during this meeting: from {{trg1.meeting.meeting_start_time}} to {{trg1.meeting.meeting_end_time}} (epoch milliseconds). A recurring meeting reuses one channel, so never include messages from before or after this meeting. Forward them to myself in Slack: find my Slack user by my email first, then send the messages to that user as a Slack direct message. If no message falls in that window, send nothing and say so.\n\n## Execution receipt\n- Begin the `response` value with a compact execution receipt of at most 1000 characters, before any report, message body, analysis, or other long-form content.\n- Start the receipt with `Outcome: SUCCESS`, `Outcome: PARTIAL`, `Outcome: NO_ACTION`, or `Outcome: FAILED`, matching the observed run result.\n- Add `Reads:` with the provider/source plus the concrete record identifiers, titles, queries, or result counts actually observed and used; write `Reads: NONE` when no read ran.\n- Add `Writes:` with one compact entry per attempted side effect: provider, operation, resolved target or recipient, provider-confirmed status, and returned identifier or URL when the tool supplies one. If no write ran, state `Writes: NONE` and the reason.\n- Add `Checks:` with every core requested section, field, constraint, and required sink marked `OK` or `MISSING` from observed evidence. Keep it terse, but never omit a failed core check to fit the receipt; for a long artifact, this checklist is the bounded proof of its contents.\n- Never claim success from intent or generated content alone, never invent receipt evidence, and never defer the only business-outcome evidence until after long-form content."
            },
